## Certification requirements for HIV case management providers

### 1) Agency/provider certification requirements
NC Medicaid requires an HIV Case Management provider to:

- have a **documented successful record of three years of providing or managing HIV Case Management programs**;
- ensure HIV Case Management services are provided by **qualified case managers** meeting the standards in Sections 6.1.1, 6.1.2, and 6.1.3;
- ensure HIV Case Management is supervised by **qualified supervisors** meeting the standards in Sections 6.1.4 and 6.1.5;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as a provider of HIV Case Management services;
- meet applicable **state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV Case Management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records** through a **quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary records** and inspect **agency operations and financial records**;
- **notify NC Medicaid or the DHHS designated contractor** of proposed changes such as ownership, name, address, telephone number, or plans for dissolution **within 30 calendar days of the proposed change and no later than five business days after the actual change**; and
- **achieve national accreditation within one year of Medicaid enrollment** from one of the designated accrediting agencies: **ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC**.

**Citation:** Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.2.1 — “Certification Requirements” (effective 2020-01-15): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

### 2) Case manager qualification requirements
The provider must ensure its HIV case managers meet one of the listed education/licensure pathways. A case manager must, for example, have one of the following:

- a **master’s degree** in a human services field;
- a **bachelor’s degree in social work**;
- a **bachelor’s degree in a human services field or related curriculum**, including at least **15 semester hours** related to social work, counseling, or public health, plus **six months of social work or counseling experience**;
- a **bachelor’s degree** plus **one year of counseling or related human services experience**; or
- applicable NC licensure as a **registered nurse, nurse practitioner, physician, physician assistant, or certified substance abuse counselor**, plus **two years of experience working in human services**.

**Citation:** Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.1.1 — “HIV Case Manager” (effective 2020-01-15): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

They also must be able to perform core case-management functions, including:

- assessment;
- beneficiary-centered goal development in the care plan;
- referral and linkage;
- monitoring of care and services; and
- documentation with personal signature attesting accuracy.

**Citation:** Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.1.2 — “Case Management Experience” (effective 2020-01-15): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

And they must possess the listed HIV-related knowledge, skills, and abilities, including documented proof within **one year of hire** of basic HIV knowledge based on current clinical practice.

**Citation:** Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.1.3 — “Knowledge, Skills and Abilities” (effective 2020-01-15): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

### 3) Supervisor qualification requirements
The provider must also ensure supervision by qualified HIV Case Management supervisors. Supervisors must meet one of several education/licensure-and-experience combinations, such as:

- a **master’s degree in a human services field** plus **one year of human services experience**;
- a **bachelor’s degree in social work** plus **two years of human services experience**;
- a qualifying **bachelor’s in a human services field** with specified coursework plus **two years of human services or public health experience**;
- a **bachelor’s degree** plus either **three years of human services experience** or **two years of human services experience and one year of supervisory experience**; or
- certain licensed professionals with either **three years of human services experience** or **two years plus one year of supervisory experience**.

Supervisors also must be able to direct and evaluate case management services, be knowledgeable in case management principles and practices, conduct analytical evaluations and reports, and apply professional knowledge of federal and state assistance programs for the HIV-positive population.

**Citations:**  
- Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.1.4 — “HIV Case Management Supervisor” (effective 2020-01-15): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)  
- Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.1.5 — “Case Management Supervisor’s Experience” (effective 2020-01-15): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

### 4) Recertification requirements
For recertification, the provider must:

- submit a **complete and signed Recertification Application** within **60 calendar days of receipt**;
- submit copies of items from Section 6.2.3 that have changed since initial certification;
- submit copies of all **HIV CM and supervisor credentials**;
- submit annual summaries of **quality improvement activities**, including **outcome metrics**; and
- submit to an **on-site recertification visit**, including review of beneficiary records or other clinical and business documentation as needed.

**Citation:** Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.2.4 — “Recertification” (effective 2020-01-15): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

Related recertification application materials also require items such as listing all case managers and supervisors with dates of employment, counties served, operating hours and after-hours emergency plan, accrediting agency information, satisfaction survey information, and Medicaid/Ryan White client counts; and they require agreement to on-site review, verification of staff credentials, supervisory monitoring, a business plan, computer capabilities, and compliance with HIPAA and record-retention rules.

**Citations:**  
- DMA-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist: [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf)  
- DMA-3158 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application, Section 4 — “Compliance”: [dma-3158 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3158.pdf)

## When can a provider be decertified?

NC Medicaid may decertify an HIV Case Management provider for any of the following reasons:

- **failure to provide core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing practices**;
- **owner conviction of a felony**;
- failure to **develop, submit, and implement a written plan of correction**, or make recommended corrections, within **30 calendar days**;
- **falsification of records**;
- **violation of beneficiary confidentiality**;
- employing **staff who do not meet Section 6.1 criteria**;
- staff failure to complete **mandatory basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- staff failure to obtain required **continuing education units (CEUs)**;
- failure to provide staff with **supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs**;
- failure to submit **required documentation** within policy or agency time frames;
- failure to provide documentation sufficient to **support billing** under Section 7.5;
- failure to **implement and enforce a quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid or its contractor of changes in agency name, director/ownership, address, or phone numbers, resulting in inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with **applicable federal and state laws, regulations, state reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to **enroll any beneficiaries within four months of certification**; or
- failure to **achieve or maintain certification requirements** under Section 6.2.

**Citation:** Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.2.5 — “Decertification” (effective 2020-01-15): [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

## Bottom line
In plain terms, an HIV Case Management provider must show:

- sufficient organizational experience,
- qualified case managers and supervisors,
- Medicaid enrollment for each site,
- accreditation within one year,
- quality-improvement and monitoring capacity,
- cooperation with audits/reviews,
- timely notice of organizational changes, and
- ongoing compliance with training, documentation, billing, and legal requirements.

A provider can be decertified not just for fraud, but also for **staff qualification problems, missed training/CEUs, poor supervision, inadequate documentation, confidentiality violations, failure to correct deficiencies, and failure to maintain certification standards**.

